%N A402293 Maximum number of regions that can be formed in the plane by drawing n non-overlapping circles.
%C A402293 The circles may touch, but may not cross each other.
%C A402293 It will appear (see FORMULA) that this sequence is essentially equal to A296515 + 2, but the subject is of sufficient interest to justify its own entry. It is also a companion to A402292.
%H A402293 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A402293/b402293.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A402293 <a href="/index/Rec#order_02">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (-2,1).
%F A402293 a(n) = max(n + 1, 3*n - 4).
%F A402293 Proof: representing the arrangement as a graph whose vertices are the circles and whose edges encode tangency, we obtain a planar simple graph on n vertices, and every planar simple graph is so representable. The number of regions created is E + 2 by Euler's formula, where E is the number of edges in the graph. For n >= 3, the maximal number of edges in the graph is 3*n - 6. Checking the small cases by hand, we obtain the formula.
%F A402293 From _Jianing Song_, Nov 21 2026: (Start)
%F A402293 G.f.: (1 + x^3 + x^4)/(1 - x)^2.
%F A402293 E.g.f.: (3*x - 4)*exp(x) + x^2/2 + 3*x + 5. (End)
%e A402293 a(4) = 8 is achieved by drawing three mutually touching equal circles and a fourth, smaller, circle that is wedged between them.
%t A402293 A402293[n_] := If[n <= 2, n + 1, 3*n - 4]; Array[A402293, 100, 0] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Nov 20 2026 *)
%t A402293 (* Alternative: *)
%t A402293 LinearRecurrence[{2, -1}, {1, 2, 3, 5, 8}, 100] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Nov 20 2026 *)
%Y A402293 Cf. A296515, A386480, A402292.
%K A402293 nonn,easy,new
